The **income tax calculator for FY 2026-27** (AY 2027-28) computes your tax liability under both the old and new tax regimes simultaneously. Enter your gross income, salary components, and eligible deductions — the calculator returns the exact tax payable under each regime and flags which one saves you more money.

**How the Section 87A rebate works in FY 2026-27:** Under the new tax regime, the Section 87A rebate cancels out all tax if your total income does not exceed ₹12,00,000. For salaried employees, the ₹75,000 standard deduction under the new regime means this zero-tax threshold effectively extends to a gross salary of ₹12,75,000. No deduction under the old regime can produce a lower tax bill than zero — so for any salaried person below this threshold, the new regime is always the right choice.

**New regime tax slabs (FY 2026-27):** The new regime was upgraded in Budget 2025. The nil-rate threshold moved to ₹4 lakh, and the slab structure above it is: 5% on ₹4L–₹8L, 10% on ₹8L–₹12L, 15% on ₹12L–₹16L, 20% on ₹16L–₹20L, 25% on ₹20L–₹24L, and 30% above ₹24L. Despite these slab rates, most incomes below ₹12L pay zero tax after the 87A rebate.

**Old regime tax slabs (FY 2026-27):** The old regime has higher rates — 5% on ₹2.5L–₹5L, 20% on ₹5L–₹10L, and 30% above ₹10L — but compensates with a full menu of deductions. The Section 87A rebate under the old regime applies if taxable income after all deductions falls below ₹5 lakh. The standard deduction is ₹50,000.

**Key deductions under the old regime:** Section 80C covers EPF, PPF, ELSS, LIC premiums, and home loan principal repayment — capped at ₹1,50,000. Section 80D adds up to ₹25,000 for your own health insurance and ₹50,000 for senior citizen parents. HRA exemption covers a significant portion of rent paid in metro or non-metro cities. Section 24b deducts up to ₹2,00,000 in home loan interest on a self-occupied property. Section 80CCD(1B) gives an additional ₹50,000 exclusively for NPS Tier 1 contributions — over and above the 80C limit. A taxpayer with full utilisation of these deductions can reduce taxable income by ₹4.5–5 lakh or more, which is why the old regime continues to win for incomes above ₹17–18 lakh.

**Surcharge for high incomes:** A surcharge applies on top of income tax for incomes above ₹50 lakh — 10% surcharge at ₹50L–₹1Cr, 15% at ₹1Cr–₹2Cr, 25% at ₹2Cr–₹5Cr, and 25% above ₹5Cr under the new regime (capped at 25% by Budget 2023 reduction). Health and education cess of 4% applies to all taxpayers regardless of regime. The calculator includes both surcharge and cess automatically.

